  "response": "# Agent context check for 04-monorepo\n\n**The repo has no agent-facing context surface.** Finding `crime_00004` (Missing Agent Context, medium severity, 0.9 confidence) shows:\n\n- no `AGENTS.md` at the repo root\n- no `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root\n- no `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`\n- no `.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md`\n- `package.json` declares the bins `tsup` and `tsup-node`, but nothing tells an agent how to build, test or invoke them.\n\nThe finding is filed against `assets/cjs_shims.js`, but that's only where the report anchored it. It applies to the whole repo, so editing that file won't clear it. Adding any one of the four files will.\n\n## What this means for agent work\n\nAn agent opening this repo starts with nothing except the code. The rest of the scan shows why that is risky here in particular:\n\n1. **Green tests are weak evidence.** Seven tests have no assertions at all (`test/example.test.ts`, `test/graphql.test.ts`, `test/tsconfig.test.ts`, and the native-node-module test in `test/index.test.ts`). Fifteen more use only weak matchers. An agent that sees a passing suite will assume its change is safe, and often it isn't.\n2. **Some build failures are logged and then dropped.** `runTypeScriptCompiler` (`src/tsc.ts:215`), `runDtsRollup` (`src/api-extractor.ts:197`) and `runRollup` (`src/rollup.ts:187`) log a message, discard the error, and let the caller see success. `jsoncParse` (`src/utils.ts:135`) returns `{}` when parsing fails. An agent won't know that a \"successful\" build may have quietly failed its dts step.\n3. **Several names hide side effects.** `build`, `buildAll`, `buildStart` and `getRollupConfig` all call `removeFiles`, so they can delete output. An agent may treat `getRollupConfig` as a pure read.\n4. **A few modules carry most of the load.** `src/utils.ts` has 18 direct and 26 transitive importers, `src/plugin.ts` has 10 direct importers, and `src/log.ts` has 9. Changes there spread across the codebase, and nothing warns about that.\n5. **No commands are documented.** Without them, agents guess the test runner, package manager and build commands, and guessing wrong wastes a cycle at best.\n\n## Proposed minimal `AGENTS.md`\n\nI wrote this only from the scan. I haven't read `package.json`, so the commands in brackets are placeholders. Replace them with the real script names before you commit. Wrong commands in an agent instruction file do more harm than none.\n\n```markdown\n# AGENTS.md\n\nInstructions for coding agents working in this repo (tsup, a TypeScript bundler).\n\n## Commands\n<!-- Verify against package.json \"scripts\" before relying on these. -->\n- Install:   `[pnpm install]`\n- Build:     `[pnpm build]`\n- Test:      `[pnpm test]`         (single file: `[pnpm test test/index.test.ts]`)\n- Typecheck: `[pnpm typecheck]`\n- CLI bins:  `tsup`, `tsup-node` (entry: `src/cli-main.ts`)\n\n## Layout\n- `src/index.ts`: public API; `build()` orchestrates the pipeline\n- `src/cli-main.ts`: CLI option parsing → `build()`\n- `src/esbuild/`: esbuild runner and plugins (postcss, svelte, swc)\n- `src/rollup.ts`, `src/tsc.ts`, `src/api-extractor.ts`: .d.ts generation paths\n- `src/plugins/`: post-build plugins (terser, swc-target, cjs-splitting)\n- `assets/`: shims injected into output; not normal source\n- `test/`: tests; `test/utils.ts` is the shared harness\n\n## Load-bearing files (read importers before editing)\n- `src/utils.ts` (18 direct importers), `src/plugin.ts` (types for 10 modules), `src/log.ts`\n\n## Gotchas\n- `build`, `buildAll`, `getRollupConfig` delete files (`removeFiles` / `clean`).\n  They are not pure.\n- dts steps (`runTypeScriptCompiler`, `runDtsRollup`, `runRollup`) log failures\n  but do not rethrow. A \"successful\" build can still be missing .d.ts output.\n  Check the output files, not only the exit code.\n- `jsoncParse` returns `{}` on bad input instead of throwing.\n- Several tests have no assertions or only weak ones. A green suite is not proof.\n  When you change behaviour, add an assertion that would fail without your change.\n\n## Conventions\n- Keep changes scoped. Don't refactor large functions (`build`, `runEsbuild`)\n  in the same PR as a behaviour change.\n- [PR / commit conventions, e.g. conventional commits]\n```\n\n**Why this is minimal but still useful:** the file's presence at the repo root is enough to clear `crime_00004`. The commands section answers the finding's specific complaint about the bins. The Gotchas section adds what you can't see from the code: hidden deletes, failures that get swallowed, and tests that don't prove much. That's the most useful thing an agent can read before it edits.\n\n## Before you land it\n\n- Fill in the bracketed commands from `package.json` and run each one once.\n- Rerun `crimes scan --format json` and check that `missing_agent_context` is gone. The total should drop from 76 to 75.\n- If you also want Claude Code to pick this up, add a `CLAUDE.md` that just says `@AGENTS.md` rather than keeping two copies.\n\nI haven't created the file because I haven't seen the repo's real scripts. If you'd like me to, I'll read `package.json` and write `AGENTS.md` with the correct commands.",
